## 11. Key Technical Highlights

### 11.1 Prompt Engineering for Saree Generation
The raw user prompt is enhanced with a prefix to ensure contextually appropriate outputs:
```
"photorealistic full-body portrait of a beautiful Indian woman model 
 wearing an elegant draped Saree, studio lighting, fashion photography, 
 {user_prompt}"
```
This ensures the AI model generates fashion-photography-style images rather than abstract textile patterns.

### 11.2 Progressive Image Loading
Images are generated **sequentially** (not in parallel) with a 1.5-second delay between requests to avoid API rate limiting. Each image is appended to the results array as it arrives using:
```javascript
setResults(prev => [...prev, design]);
```
This gives users **instant visual feedback** — they see the first image within 3–5 seconds rather than waiting 20+ seconds for all four.

### 11.3 Client-Side Cart Architecture
The cart is stored purely in React `useState` (not localStorage, not database). This design choice:
- ✅ Eliminates unnecessary API calls during browsing
- ✅ Keeps the UI snappy with zero network latency for add/remove
- ✅ Simplifies the backend (only one write: the final order)
- ⚠️ Trade-off: Cart is lost on page refresh (acceptable for AI-generated designs)

### 11.4 MongoDB Aggregation for Analytics
The `/api/admin/stats` endpoint uses MongoDB's **Aggregation Pipeline** to compute `totalSales`:
```javascript
const salesData = await Order.aggregate([
  { $group: { _id: null, totalSales: { $sum: "$totalAmount" } } }
]);
```

### 11.5 Vite Reverse Proxy for API Security
The `vite.config.js` configures a dev-server proxy that:
- Rewrites `/api/*` requests to `https://api.freepik.com/*`
- Injects the `x-freepik-api-key` header server-side
- This keeps the API key **out of the browser's network tab**, preventing key theft

### 11.6 Scroll-Triggered Animations
Each major section on the landing page is wrapped with `react-intersection-observer`:
```javascript
const { ref: heroRef, inView: heroIsVisible } = useInView({ 
  triggerOnce: false, 
  threshold: 0.1 
});
```
When `heroIsVisible` becomes `true`, the CSS class `is-visible` is applied, triggering a `fade-in` CSS animation. Setting `triggerOnce: false` means animations replay when scrolling back up.

